WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.522
A three sixty camera films the whole room at once.

00:00:03.522 --> 00:00:07.451
So why does a good pipeline cut every frame into ten small pictures?

00:00:10.582 --> 00:00:11.652
Hi, I'm Saffi!

00:00:11.652 --> 00:00:16.643
Spirula Studio takes raw video all the way to Gaussian splats in one program.

00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:19.495
Today, how it handles a three sixty camera.

00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:21.760
Chapter one.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:22.950
Rewritten, not wrapped.

00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:31.184
Most all in one splat tools are a launcher around other people's programs.

00:00:31.184 --> 00:00:33.103
Here, each one was written again.

00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:37.863
COLMAP became a structure from motion module on Vulkan, porting its algorithms

00:00:37.863 --> 00:00:39.672
and defaults, not its code.

00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:43.001
PyTorch became a small inference runtime written in Slang.

00:00:43.421 --> 00:00:46.686
The video decoders are its own, running on the graphics card.

00:00:47.106 --> 00:00:51.618
And the segmentation model that masks out the operator runs as compute shaders.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.582
This is the desktop app.

00:00:54.582 --> 00:00:58.260
The same trainer drives it and the command line, so a cloud

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.406
box runs exactly what your laptop runs.

00:01:01.842 --> 00:01:02.487
Chapter two.

00:01:02.487 --> 00:01:03.778
A sphere into views.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.127
The easy route hands structure from motion the stitched panorama.

00:01:12.127 --> 00:01:16.972
Both lenses become one camera, downsampled to four point four pixels per degree.

00:01:17.392 --> 00:01:19.887
Spirula cuts views that each read one lens.

00:01:19.887 --> 00:01:23.630
A face holds sixteen point seven pixels per degree, and a rig

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.813
ties the views of a frame together.

00:01:27.825 --> 00:01:29.739
Here is the whole sphere, unrolled.

00:01:29.739 --> 00:01:32.609
A GoPro MAX does not stitch its two lenses.

00:01:32.609 --> 00:01:36.118
They meet down this dashed line, with real parallax across it.

00:01:36.538 --> 00:01:37.929
So Spirula cuts per lens.

00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:41.268
The front lens gets the face on its axis, plus the near

00:01:41.268 --> 00:01:42.660
half of its four neighbours.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.149
The back lens gets the same five.

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:48.696
Every view reads one lens, so each is a true pinhole camera.

00:01:49.116 --> 00:01:52.528
Only five point nine percent of the sphere is seen twice.

00:01:52.528 --> 00:01:56.249
The views of one frame share almost no features, so matches across

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:57.490
time hold them together.

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.993
A MAX 2 is already stitched, so it gets six faces

00:02:00.993 --> 00:02:02.114
on a tilted cube.

00:02:02.114 --> 00:02:06.037
Kept level, my check finds about two percent of the sphere in no view.

00:02:07.358 --> 00:02:10.141
Every camera model is a rule for where a ray lands.

00:02:10.561 --> 00:02:13.731
A pinhole puts a ray at the focal length times the

00:02:13.731 --> 00:02:14.883
tangent of its angle.

00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:16.612
At ninety degrees that is infinite.

00:02:17.032 --> 00:02:20.820
An equidistant fisheye grows with the angle itself, so a lens of

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.661
about two hundred degrees still fits on the sensor.

00:02:25.342 --> 00:02:27.445
A dual fisheye file is different again.

00:02:27.445 --> 00:02:29.849
It holds two fisheye tracks, one per lens.

00:02:30.269 --> 00:02:33.776
Each track is already one central camera, so each gets a fisheye

00:02:33.776 --> 00:02:34.945
model of its own.

00:02:34.945 --> 00:02:35.529
No cutting.

00:02:35.949 --> 00:02:39.230
Structure from motion is told the two lenses are a rig, back

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.964
to back, and solves one pose for the whole frame.

00:02:42.384 --> 00:02:44.755
Training renders the fisheye directly by default.

00:02:44.755 --> 00:02:48.481
It splits each lens into five faces only when you ask.

00:02:49.925 --> 00:02:53.607
In the project's own reel: a fisheye frame of a room, masked,

00:02:53.607 --> 00:02:54.835
then the cameras solved.

00:02:54.835 --> 00:02:57.289
The timings on screen are the project's claims.

00:02:58.717 --> 00:02:59.346
Chapter three.

00:02:59.346 --> 00:03:00.917
From splats to a building.

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.518
A building model needs metres and gravity.

00:03:07.518 --> 00:03:09.720
Spirula has four ways to get them.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.959
The camera's own telemetry, read from the video file.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.791
Printed markers, new in October and still marked experimental.

00:03:17.211 --> 00:03:20.834
A distance you measured on site, between two points you pick in the editor.

00:03:21.254 --> 00:03:25.081
Or a laser scan, which is metres by definition and already level.

00:03:26.925 --> 00:03:28.856
The mesh starts from the trained Gaussians.

00:03:29.865 --> 00:03:34.248
Each one gives seven points: its centre, and two along each of its axes.

00:03:34.668 --> 00:03:38.296
Those points are joined into tetrahedra, and each corner is called inside

00:03:38.296 --> 00:03:41.622
only if every camera's line of sight to it is blocked.

00:03:42.042 --> 00:03:46.705
The surface is cut where that occupancy crosses one half, then cleaned and textured.

00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:51.003
Reading the code also showed what nobody has measured.

00:03:51.423 --> 00:03:54.910
Nobody, the project included, has said how many millimetres off a

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:56.178
wall the mesh lands.

00:03:56.598 --> 00:04:01.249
The finished reconstructions are not yet scored against COLMAP on a public dataset.

00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:03.862
And I read the code without running the binary.

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:08.487
So cut the sphere per lens, and tell the solver the rig.

00:04:08.487 --> 00:04:11.438
Then model from the dense points, and show people the splat.

00:04:14.108 --> 00:04:14.781
To recap.

00:04:14.781 --> 00:04:15.790
Rewritten, not wrapped.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.482
Views per lens, held together by a rig.

00:04:18.482 --> 00:04:21.174
Points for the modeller, splats for everyone else.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.721
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:24.328
I'm Saffi.

00:04:24.328 --> 00:04:24.632
Bye!
